WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.586 --> 00:00:06.910
So we have--

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.170
the hypothetical situation where
we have discovered this chick

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.290
weight data set that
exists built into R. It's

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.130
one of the built-in data sets.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.185
So we have chick weight.

00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:22.000
It's got weight,
time, chick, diet,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.180
and maybe we've made some
plots with that stuff.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.670
So we look at chick weight
over time, nice line plot.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.070
Maybe we've looked
at the density,

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:38.350
and we say I want to build
a Shiny app with this.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.890
I've got these nice
plots, and I want

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.780
to put them in a
Shiny app, but I'm

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.550
dreading that aspect of
going in and setting up

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:54.340
that whole UI because
that's just code

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.720
that I haven't written before.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.470
It's annoying to
remember how to write it.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.360
So that is kind of a use
case that the UI Editor

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.110
is attempting to
make a lot easier.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.970
So we can go in
this project that we

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.620
have that's empty, except for
these chickPlots.R script that

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.240
has those plots.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.210
We load the Shiny UI Editor,
and then we say Launch Editor,

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.860
and we give it this application.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.330
We call it chick dash plots.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.470
You notice we don't actually
have chick dash plots in here,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.420
and what it's going
to do is recognize

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.910
that doesn't exist yet and
ask you a few questions

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.450
about creating it.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.710
So you say I want to start
a new app from a template.

00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:36.790
Choose which template right now.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.165
We don't have that
many, so we're

00:01:38.165 --> 00:01:39.910
going to do a single
file template that

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.170
is the Geyser app.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.320
Run that.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.040
And now the UI Editor
is up and running,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.910
and it's running just this
simple Geyser app just

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.550
with two plots instead
of the typical one,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.650
but it's an app that
you're well familiar with.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.710
So we can actually go in.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.620
We see now that there is
chick dash plots in here,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.140
and we can click into
that, and we see an app.R

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.470
and we can open that up.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:07.990
And this is the code that the
UI Editor is currently running.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:13.120
And so we can see that if
we want to change stuff,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.220
like say we have
our header here,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.480
and it is spanning the whole
top, we drag it over here.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.420
It updates, and we see now
the header is only spanning

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.780
the first column there.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.250
And so any update that
you make in the UI Editor

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:32.450
is automatically updated
in the app code itself.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.040
So that's nice, but what if
you wanted to actually adjust

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.280
what is being plotted?

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.130
A lot of this fun stuff in
Shiny apps is doing the plots.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.650
And so we already
have render plots here

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.750
for distPlot and bluePlot.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:51.670
And so what we can do is take
some of our chick plot data,

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:57.190
and grab this code and
replace in our app.R

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:04.400
this distPlot with that
code, and we can save it.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:05.980
And you'll see that
there's an error.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:06.730
We can check this.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.120
It says it could not find
plot function ggplot.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.790
So we actually need
to load ggplot.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:18.430
So say library,
ggplot2, save that.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.560
Another error.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.570
Object chick not found.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.200
So let's go back in
here one more time.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.340
Filter out.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.920
This is a classic error.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.460
Filter is one of those functions
that exists in the dplyr

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.800
and also in the base,
so we need to actually

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.084
explicitly load dplyer.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.370
Now we've been making
these changes directly

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.060
to our server looking at the
errors that are coming through

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.230
in the UI Editor.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:53.160
And now we actually have that
plot up and running in our app.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.680
And so one of the things
we probably want to change

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.430
is to not distPlot.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.130
So we're going to
call this weights.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.270
So we've updated.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.280
Now it's not there
because it's looking

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.900
for the plot output
of weights, which

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.970
we don't have because
we haven't renamed it.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:09.570
Go in here.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.590
Rename it to weights.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.750
Save it, and it's back.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.626
And so we can
continue doing this

00:04:15.626 --> 00:04:16.959
for a bunch of different things.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.730
So let's go take this
distribution plot here,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:25.140
and let's replace a
bluePlot with that.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.910
BluePlot-- that paste
in there, save it.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:31.570
Then there.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.380
It's a little bit
confusingly called bluePlot,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.290
even though we have some
orange-red distribution.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.320
So let's do the same thing here.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.240
Call this distributions,
or maybe call it dist.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.500
Once again, it's gone.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.610
Let's go fix it.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.900
This, save it, back.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.380
So you could see that
we could just continue

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.670
to make our changes here.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:02.020
Let's call this chick weights.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.740
Center, code is
continuing to update.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.490
So one thing that we
might want to do, also

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.290
is we have this number of bins--

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.960
which isn't doing anything
for us right now--

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.260
but we're filtering to a given
number of chicks, in this case,

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.240
it's six.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.940
So what we might want to
do is take this slider

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:26.150
and repurpose it to decide
that filter number for us.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:31.960
So let's change
it to num chicks.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:38.710
Call it chicks to plot.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.750
And I don't think
we have that many.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.645
Let's maybe set the
min to 1, max 2.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.490
And start at a value that
is reasonable, say 5.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:55.510
And let's set a step value
so we don't have incremental.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.160
Let's say step value of 1.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.540
So we can go in here.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.800
We see that we actually have
this slider up and running,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.570
but it's not hooked
up to anything.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:07.390
But we now know
that we have created

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.130
an input ID of num chicks.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.900
We can just copy that.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.160
Come over here,
say input, input.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.500
Dollar sign num chicks.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.050
Erase that, copy
this, get rid of that

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.240
because we don't
need it anymore.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.950
Save.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:30.280
So, in theory, this
will now change,

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.750
but the classic factor
problem is getting us.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.020
So it's not exactly
doing what we

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.380
want because we're doing
chick less than input

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.188
number of chicks.

00:06:43.188 --> 00:06:44.980
It's not actually giving
us the same value.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.690
And this has actually
happened before.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:47.350
We did this.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.120
Just we didn't notice it.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.250
We didn't count the lines.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:54.790
So we should go
into chick weight,

00:06:54.790 --> 00:07:04.090
mutate chick, and start
numeric as well as an integer.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:11.920
That might be right.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:12.550
There we go.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:16.060
Now it's all fixed, but
we can do all of that.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.570
We can mess with our code,
we can update our plots,

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:22.390
and all we have to do is just
go back and forth between the UI

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:26.090
Editor and the code.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.840
And so the idea
is you don't have

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.670
to sit there and fiddle
with this grid card.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:33.430
What's the argument name?

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:34.780
Where do I nest this?

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.722
I want to put it over here.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.180
You don't need to
worry about that.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:43.360
You can just focus on
writing good data science,

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:49.030
doing correct plots, doing
your dplyr pipes properly.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:50.890
We want you to focus
on those issues which

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:54.880
are straightforward and
not this crazy world that

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.560
is, how do you get your text
to align properly in your UI.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.320
How do you make your
header a little bit taller

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.150
than it was before?

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.950
That stuff that
doesn't influence how

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:09.470
good are as a data scientist.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.670
So we're trying to make
that as easy as possible.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:18.670
Before we talked about
if we had a table, right?

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:23.660
So we have this code here.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:36.133
So take that down here and
use output table [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.850
We actually put
that table in here.

00:09:00.282 --> 00:09:07.440
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.780
So we go into this grid
card that has nothing in it,

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:15.390
and we say gt.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.173
And it's like gt output.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:30.322
So this idea of we
have a table in here--

00:09:30.322 --> 00:09:34.310
let me make it bigger so
we can see the table--

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.060
we have this table in
here, but the UI editor

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.790
doesn't support it,
doesn't have it,

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:43.190
doesn't know about
gt tables yet.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:47.690
And so what it does is it gives
you this little gray box that

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.730
says, hey, I have
this UI function

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:51.110
that I don't know about.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.130
Here's the code.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.130
You can't edit it because
I don't know about it,

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:58.070
but it is treated just
like any other node.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.720
So you could take this
and put it up here.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.420
Drag this down.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.340
That's [INAUDIBLE]
your tables in there.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:13.940
So you can treat it just like
you would any other node,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.500
but it doesn't know about it.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.450
And so the idea there
is that we don't limit.

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:26.060
If you have some cool
custom function or just

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.790
a function that's
not included yet,

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.437
it's not going to make it so
you can't use the UI Editor.

00:10:33.437 --> 00:10:36.020
It just means that you won't be
able to use the nice UI Editor

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.760
settings panel to change
it, but you can still

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.710
lay out your app and
everything and place it

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:48.380
where you want within the
app and then see what it is.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.420
So that will help.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:54.260
Ideally, in the future,
every possible thing

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.270
that you put in your
Shiny app is known,

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.730
but I don't think that's
ever going to be the case.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:03.480
And so this is how that
is treated in the editor.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:08.680
So that's important to know.

